in @prism

in @io
in @io/bytes

# @io/crypto
#
# Hash + signature primitives over @io/bytes. Two hash functions
# (sha1, sha256) and one signature scheme (ed25519, sign + verify).
# Lives under @io by construction so glass_wall (#79) validates
# trivially.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-kintsugi.md
# §"What stays in @io permanently": SHA-1 specifically is named as
# needed for git interop and remains a permanent @io resident.
# SHA-256 lives at the boundary because the canonical implementation
# is vendor crypto (sha2 crate's intrinsics dispatch); cross_wall
# (#80) may someday pull a structurally-verified Rust implementation
# across, but for the bootstrap era SHA-256 stays @io. Ed25519 is
# permanent @io — the underlying field arithmetic is opaque vendor
# code by necessity (constant-time disciplines are crate-internal).
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-26-portal-as-io-socket-over-content-
# addressed-subspace.md §"Open question 1: Authentication": the
# natural answer is portal authentication by signing the subspace
# OID with the shard's ed25519 key; receiver verifies against the
# shard's published public key. ed25519_sign / ed25519_verify here
# is the substrate that machinery uses.
#
# Important architectural note: @fragmentation's existing SHA-256
# usage (the content-addressing primitive that names every crystal
# in the store) can in a follow-up commit be re-routed to call
# @io/crypto.sha256 here, collapsing two SHA-256 surfaces into one.
# That re-routing is OUT OF SCOPE for this commit — it would touch
# the crystal-store boundary which is hot, well-tested, and pinned
# by oid_smoke.rs's OID values. This commit only declares the
# primitive; the @fragmentation re-route is a separate landing.
#
# Bootstrap-side dispatch (when @mirror/serve lands the tournament-
# to-bootstrap path):
#
#   sha1           → `sha1` crate
#   sha256         → `sha2` crate (already in Cargo.toml)
#   ed25519_sign   → `ed25519-dalek` crate
#   ed25519_verify → `ed25519-dalek` crate
#
# No Cargo.toml addition in this commit. The new crate pulls
# (sha1, ed25519-dalek) land with the dispatch landing. sha2 is
# already present per Cargo.toml (it backs hash.rs's CoincidenceHash).
#
# Sibling grammars under @io: @io/bytes (the carrier type),
# @io/random (entropy for key generation), @io/encode (rendering
# hashes and signatures for display/transport).

grammar @io/crypto {

  # ── sha1: SHA-1 hash ────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # 20-byte SHA-1 digest. Present for git interop — git's content
  # store uses SHA-1 (transitioning to SHA-256 but not there yet),
  # so the bootstrap needs to compute SHA-1 to interop with git
  # plumbing (hash-object, cat-file, etc). Total; every byte input
  # has a canonical SHA-1.
  sha1(b: bytes) -> bytes { \ }

  # ── sha256: SHA-256 hash ──────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # 32-byte SHA-256 digest. The hash function @fragmentation uses
  # under the hood for content-addressing; this declaration makes
  # the surface explicit so a future commit can re-route
  # @fragmentation's internal SHA-256 calls through @io/crypto.
  # Total.
  sha256(b: bytes) -> bytes { \ }

  # ── ed25519_sign: produce a signature ──────────────────────────
  #
  # Sign `msg` under the ed25519 secret key `key`. Output is 64
  # bytes (R || s). The key encoding follows RFC 8032: 32 bytes
  # for the secret scalar (the typical "secret key" surface from
  # @io/random.bytes(32)). Total at the signature layer; the
  # signature is deterministic given (key, msg).
  ed25519_sign(key: bytes, msg: bytes) -> bytes { \ }

  # ── ed25519_verify: verify a signature ─────────────────────────
  #
  # Verify `sig` against (pub_key, msg). Returns imperfect:
  # Success() iff the signature is valid; Failure with diagnostic
  # iff malformed inputs (wrong length pub_key/sig, non-canonical
  # encoding) or invalid signature. The two failure modes — invalid
  # input vs invalid signature — are distinguished in the diagnostic.
  ed25519_verify(pub_key: bytes, sig: bytes, msg: bytes) -> imperfect { \ }
}

out sha1
out sha256
out ed25519_sign
out ed25519_verify
out @io/crypto
